body:
I think that this this is a bit overcomplicated. 

Before the flow just returned early by 
```
	// If Stop already set killed, leave status as killed.
	if l.status == StatusKilled {
		return
	}
```


In the current version, a magic value 128 is introduced. Is there a way to simplify this flow and set the exit code correctly without relying on a hard-coded magic number?

Related to  https://github.com/kkloberdanz/teleport-challenge/pull/7#discussion_r2813591858

As a curiosity, I took a look at the bash source code to see how bash handles this, and it appears to take the same approach as the Go code I pushed, i.e., check if the process was signaled, then calculate the code by adding 128 to the signal number.

https://cgit.git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/bash.git/tree/jobs.c#n2961

```c
int
process_exit_status (WAIT status)
{
  if (WIFSIGNALED (status))
    return (128 + WTERMSIG (status));
  else if (WIFSTOPPED (status) == 0)
    return (WEXITSTATUS (status));
  else
    return (EXECUTION_SUCCESS);
}
```

I took a crack at replicating this behavior with a small C program to see what we get. I see that glibc gives us an exit code of `0` for a process that received a `SIGTERM`.  We can use `WTERMSIG` to get the signal that was used to terminate the process, which gives us 15 for a `SIGTERM`. When I compile and run like so, I see the behavior below:
```sh
$ cc main.c && ./a.out
Child killed by signal 15, exit code: 0
```

This demonstrates that the behavior of 128 + signal_number is not coming from the OS (i.e., it is not coming from either libc or the kernel).

Using this for `main.c`.
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(void) {
    pid_t pid = fork();

    if (pid < 0) {
        /* fork failed */
        perror("fork");
        return 1;
    } if (pid == 0) {
        /* child: sleep until signaled */
        pause();
    } else {
        /* parent */
        int status;
        kill(pid, SIGTERM);

        waitpid(pid, &status, 0);

        if (WIFSIGNALED(status)) {
            int sig = WTERMSIG(status);
            int exit_code = WEXITSTATUS(status); /* This gets the exit code for a process. Typically meaningless for a process terminated by a signal, but works as an example of the behavior we are seeing. */
            printf(
                "Child killed by signal %d, exit code: %d\n",
                sig,
                exit_code
            );
        } else if (WIFEXITED(status)) {
            printf("Child exited with code %d\n", WEXITSTATUS(status));
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

So from what I can see, the behavior of 128 + signal_number is a POSIX user space convention, which is implemented within the shell itself instead of the kernel. It appears that the Go standard library deviates from this behavior, and instead returns a `-1` for any process that exited due to a signal.

In summary, it appears in order to match the behavior that was requested above, i.e., 128 + signal_number for the exit code, then this would need to be calculated.

As a final demonstration of Go's behavior for assigning `-1` to processes terminated by a signal, if we run the program below, we see this behavior occurring:

```sh
$ go run ./main.go
started process with pid 541365
sent SIGTERM
exit code: -1
```

```Go
package main

import (
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"os"
	"os/exec"
	"syscall"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	cmd := exec.Command("sleep", "30")
	if err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "failed to start process: %v\n", err)
		os.Exit(1)
	}
	fmt.Printf("started process with pid %d\n", cmd.Process.Pid)

	go func() {
		time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)
		cmd.Process.Signal(syscall.SIGTERM)
		fmt.Println("sent SIGTERM")
	}()

	err := cmd.Wait()
	var exitErr *exec.ExitError
	if errors.As(err, &exitErr) {
		fmt.Printf("exit code: %d\n", exitErr.ExitCode())
	} else if err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "wait error: %v\n", err)
	} else {
		fmt.Println("exit code: 0")
	}
}
